I was thinking we could use embodied imitation learning to learn a procedure-conditioned value function over surgical trajectories, as a proxy for successful subgoal completion and trajectory quality. I’ve also been working on unifying open-ended embodied learning with robot imitation learning, where automatic curriculum generation could help fill gaps in the demonstration data by generating or selecting examples in underrepresented parts of the task space.HoloHub – Explore The Holoscan EcosystemProcedural Narrator (latest) - HoloHub – Explore The Holoscan EcosystemDisplays a replay or V4L2 clinical video with ongoing temporal narration from an OpenAI-compatible multimodal reasoner.HoloHub
One concept I think could be interesting is to use Cosmos-Nano as the WAM and decompose behavior into reusable capability components, then learn how to recombine those capabilities during online post-training for compositional adaptation / robust OOD generalization. We could use expert surgical demonstrations and scores to learn an expert-proxy value function evaluator that estimates successful subgoal completion and trajectory quality. This could then provide performance scores, highlight specific maneuvers or deviations, and generate language-grounded recommendations that new surgeons could use to improve.

We could also pair this with cuRobo -- I already have a working manipulation agent using it -- to generate controlled trajectories around known modeled constraint-violation boundaries. That would give us explicit nominal vs. violation examples to test whether the learned subgoal/value representation actually responds correctly, while automatic curriculum generation could target missing capability combinations or weak regions of the task space.

So essentially: embodied imitation learning from task video for expert review: deviation / constraint-violation detection, performance evaluation, and language-grounded improvement recommendations.


